why: operator instruction 2026-09-09 — close channel-default-adaptive,
  flush-premium and growing-capacity-semantics. Each closes a
  different way and none of them by assertion:

  1. channel-default-adaptive is ANSWERED and only the box is
     unticked. The default became `growing` (17404a4e); `adaptive` as
     a default was refuted by measurement — it splits its capacity
     across parts, so a lone producer reads 1 119 against the ring's
     169.
  2. flush-premium needs a MEASUREMENT, not a decision. §6b says the
     flusher costs 9% over the chunked merge and the re-measure said
     30%. Both lanes carry wide bars (okayChunkedFlush read ±96.8 in
     the refresh), so the first question is whether 30% and 9% are
     even distinguishable here. Enough rounds to answer that, then
     close it as a real premium or as noise.
  3. growing-capacity-semantics is now a NAMING bug rather than a
     behaviour one, and that is the fix. Since growing-part-sizing,
     `growing(capacity, parts)` gives `capacity` PER PART — which is
     exactly what `adaptive.parts(n).each(c)` gives. The two builders
     already agree; only the parameter names disagree, and that
     mismatch cost this session two false starts.

what: tick (1) with the evidence, measure (2) and close it either way,
  rename `growing`'s parameter to say what it means for (3). Two
  callers, both in TestGrowing, both positional.

predict: for (2) — the bars overlap and the premium is not
  distinguishable from the old 9%. DISQUALIFYING: if the bars are
